.TH BGREP 1 "bgrep 0.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bgrep \- grep for binary data
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bgrep
.RI [ OPTION ]\|.\|.\|.
.I PATTERN
.RI [ FILE ]\|.\|.\|.
.br
.B bgrep
.RI [ OPTION ]\|.\|.\|.
.RB { \-e " \fIPATTERN\fR | " \-f " \fIFILE\fR }\|.\|.\|."
.RI [ FILE ]\|.\|.\|.
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bgrep
searches the named input
.IR FILE s
(or standard input if none are given, or if a
.I FILE
is
.BR \- )
for byte sequences that match
.IR PATTERN ,
and prints the offset and contents of each match.
Where
.BR grep (1)
works on lines of text,
.B bgrep
works on arbitrary binary data: a known header, magic number or record
layout can be located inside a file by describing it as hexadecimal.
.PP
Matches never overlap; after a match the search resumes at the first
candidate position after its end (see \fBBITSTREAM MODEL\fP).
When several patterns match at the same position, the one given first wins.
.PP
The command-line interface and this page borrow heavily from
.BR grep (1)
so that the two feel alike.
.
.SH "BITSTREAM MODEL"
.B bgrep
sees its input as a stream of bits and divides it into "bytes" of
.B \-\^\-input\-byte
bits (default 8; 1 to 128).  Packed data such as 6, 7, 10, 12 or 14 bit
samples can thus be searched and displayed directly.
.IP \(bu 3
Bits are taken in order, most significant bit of each file byte first
(the default) or least significant first with
.BR \-\^\-lsb\-first .
.IP \(bu 3
The first input byte starts at the beginning of the input, or at
.B \-\^\-starting\-offset
(which counts input bytes).
.IP \(bu 3
A match may begin every
.B \-\^\-align
bits counted from there.  By default that is every input byte, so matches are
byte aligned.  A smaller alignment finds data that is not aligned to the
byte grid;
.B \-\^\-half\-byte
is
.BR \-\^\-align=4 .
After a match, scanning resumes at the next alignment position past its end.
.IP \(bu 3
Offsets are reported in input bytes (plus a remainder in bits, written
.BR + BITS ,
when a match is off the input-byte grid), or in bits with
.BR \-\^\-bit\-offset .
.IP \(bu 3
Matched bits (and context) are displayed regrouped into
.B \-\^\-output\-byte
bit units (default 8; 1 to 128).  With the defaults this reproduces the
original file bytes.  A final unit shorter than the others shows only the
digits it covers, with a \fB~\fP appended if its length is not a whole
number of hex digits.
.PP
Examples: three file bytes \fBab cd ef\fP are the two 12-bit input bytes
\fBabc def\fP;  the bytes \fB41 42 43\fP are the four 6-bit input bytes
\fB10 14 09 03\fP; GSM 7-bit packed text is searched with
\fB\-\^\-lsb\-first \-\^\-input\-byte=7\fP and shown as plain septet values with
\fB\-\^\-output\-byte=7\fP.
.
.SH "PATTERN SYNTAX"
A pattern describes input bytes, written in hexadecimal.
.SS "Default (\-F): hex digits and wildcards"
.PP
Each input byte is written as ceil(BITS/4) hex digits holding its numeric
value; for the default 8-bit byte that is two digits.  A period
.RB ( . )
stands for any single hex digit.
White space is ignored.
When the byte width is not a multiple of 4 the leading digit holds only the
remaining bits, so it must be small enough to fit (for 6-bit bytes,
\fB0\fP to \fB3\fP), and a \fB.\fP there matches just those bits.
.PP
The pattern must be a whole number of input bytes (for 8-bit bytes, an even
number of digits), unless
.B \-\^\-align
or
.B \-\^\-half\-byte
is given, which also permits a short final byte (for 8-bit bytes, an odd
number of digits; with other widths, hex digits only when the width is a
multiple of 4).  A short final byte is not allowed with
.BR \-\^\-lsb\-first .
.PP
.nf
.RS
68 65 6c 6c 6f      the bytes "hello"
de ad be ef         DEADBEEF
ff d8 ff ..         a JPEG start marker followed by any byte
4. 50               a byte 0x40\-0x4f followed by 0x50
abc def             two 12\-bit bytes (with \-\^\-input\-byte=12)
.RE
.fi
.SS "Binary patterns (\-\^\-binary)"
With
.B \-\^\-binary
every symbol is a single bit:
.BR 0 ,
.B 1
or
.B .
(any bit).  White space is ignored.  This is the natural notation for
byte widths that are not a multiple of 4:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\-\^\-binary \-\^\-input\-byte=6 '010100 001001'    the 6\-bit bytes 14 and 09
\-\^\-binary \-\^\-align=1 '1111 0000 .....'       a bit pattern anywhere
.RE
.fi
.SS "Regular expressions (\-G, \-E)"
With
.B \-G
or
.B \-E
the pattern is a POSIX basic or extended regular expression
.RB ( regex (7))
applied to the data rendered as text: each input byte as ceil(BITS/4)
lowercase hex digits (or, with
.BR \-\^\-binary ,
BITS characters
.B 0
and
.BR 1 ),
with no separators.
White space in the pattern is removed and matching is case-insensitive.
The period matches one text character (a hex digit, or one bit), so
.B ..
matches one 8-bit byte, and the full power of regular expressions is
available:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\-E '504b(0304|0506)'           ZIP local header or end record
\-E 'ff(d8|d9)'                 JPEG start or end marker
\-E '00{8,}'                    eight or more zero bytes
\-E '^7f454c46'                 an ELF header at the very start
.RE
.fi
.PP
Matches must start and end on an input-byte boundary; a match that does not
is skipped and the search continues one character further on.  A pattern
such as
.B 8656
therefore does not match inside
.BR 6865\^6c6c ,
unless
.B \-\^\-align
(or \fB\-\^\-half\-byte\fP) permits it: matches then need only start on the
alignment grid.  That combination is not supported together with
.B \-\^\-lsb\-first
in regex mode.
.PP
Regex mode reads each input completely into memory, using about three times
its size (about eight times with \fB\-\^\-binary\fP).  The default mode
streams its input in constant memory.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Pattern selection"
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\^\-fixed
Interpret patterns as hex digits and wildcards (the default).
.TP
.BR \-G ", " \-\^\-basic\-regexp
Interpret patterns as POSIX basic regular expressions.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\^\-extended\-regexp
Interpret patterns as POSIX extended regular expressions.
.TP
.B \-\^\-binary
Patterns are written one bit per symbol (\fB0\fP, \fB1\fP, \fB.\fP).
.TP
.BI \-e " PATTERN" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-regexp=" PATTERN
Use
.I PATTERN
as a pattern.  May be given multiple times.  When
.B \-e
or
.B \-f
is used, no positional
.I PATTERN
is expected.
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-file=" FILE
Read patterns from
.IR FILE ,
one per line; blank lines are ignored.  Use
.B \-
for standard input.  Unlike earlier versions, \fB\-f\fP does
.I not
name a file to search; list files as ordinary arguments.
.SS "General output control"
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\^\-count
Print only the number of matches in each file.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\^\-files\-with\-matches
Print only the names of files that contain a match.  Scanning of a file
stops at its first match.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\^\-files\-without\-match
Print only the names of files that contain no match.
.TP
.BI \-m " NUM" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-max\-count=" NUM
Stop reading a file after
.I NUM
matches.  0 (the default) means no limit.  Decimal, hexadecimal
(leading \fB0x\fP) and octal (leading \fB0\fP) are accepted.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\^\-quiet ", " \-\^\-silent
Write nothing; exit immediately with status 0 as soon as any match is
found, even if an error occurred earlier.
.SS "Output line prefix control"
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\^\-byte\-offset
Print the offset of each match before it (the default).  The base is
described under \fBNumber formats\fP below.
.TP
.B \-\^\-no\-byte\-offset
Do not print the offset.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\^\-with\-filename
Prefix each output line with the file name.  This is the default when
more than one file is searched.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\^\-no\-filename
Never prefix output lines with the file name.
.TP
.BI \-\^\-label= LABEL
Use
.I LABEL
instead of \(lq(stdin)\(rq as the name of standard input.
.SS "Input control and bitstream options"
.TP
.BI \-s " OFFSET" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-starting\-offset=" OFFSET
Begin searching at input byte
.I OFFSET
(default 0).  Offsets are still reported from the start of the file.
Seekable files are seeked; pipes are read and discarded.
.TP
.BI \-\^\-input\-byte= BITS
Treat the input as bytes of
.I BITS
bits (1 to 128, default 8).  See \fBBITSTREAM MODEL\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\^\-output\-byte= BITS
Show matches regrouped into bytes of
.I BITS
bits (1 to 128, default 8).  Reported offsets are not affected.
.TP
.BI \-\^\-align= BITS
Allow matches to begin every
.I BITS
bits counted from the starting offset (default: the input byte size).
Also lets patterns end part-way through an input byte.
.TP
.B \-\^\-half\-byte
Shorthand for \fB\-\^\-align=4\fP: match on nibble boundaries.
Finding \fB8656\fP inside \fB68 65 6c\fP reports offset \fB2+4\fP and
prints \fB86 56\fP.
.TP
.B \-\^\-lsb\-first
Take the bits of each file byte starting from the least significant bit.
This is how many packed formats (such as GSM 7-bit text) are laid out.
.TP
.B \-\^\-msb\-first
Take bits most significant first (the default).
.TP
.B \-\^\-bit\-offset
Report offsets as bit positions rather than input bytes.
.TP
.B \-\^\-no\-bit\-offset
Report offsets in input bytes (the default).
.SS "Offset reporting"
In the case of several of these options, the last one given wins.
.TP
.B \-\^\-first\-byte
Report the offset of the first byte of the match (default).
.TP
.B \-\^\-first\-byte\-with\-prefix
Report the offset of the first byte shown, including leading context bytes.
.TP
.B \-\^\-last\-byte
Report the offset of the last byte of the match.
.TP
.B \-\^\-last\-byte\-with\-suffix
Report the offset of the last byte shown, including trailing context bytes.
.SS "Number formats"
Offsets are in input bytes.  The remainder, when a match is not on the input
byte grid, follows as \fB+\fP and a decimal number of bits.
Numeric option arguments (\fB\-m\fP, \fB\-s\fP, \fB\-A\fP, \fB\-B\fP, \fB\-P\fP,
\fB\-S\fP, \fB\-O\fP, \fB\-I\fP, \fB\-g\fP, \fB\-p\fP, \fB\-\^\-input\-byte\fP,
\fB\-\^\-output\-byte\fP, \fB\-\^\-align\fP) are interpreted in the
base chosen here, wherever these options appear on the command line.
.TP
.B \-\^\-auto\-mode
Arguments are decimal unless prefixed with \fB0x\fP (hexadecimal) or
\fB0\fP (octal).  Offsets are printed in hexadecimal and counts (\fB\-c\fP)
in decimal.  This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\^\-hex\-mode
Arguments are hexadecimal (no \fB0x\fP needed) and offsets and counts are
printed in hexadecimal.
.TP
.B \-\^\-oct\-mode
Arguments are octal; offsets and counts are printed in octal.
.TP
.B \-\^\-dec\-mode
Arguments are decimal; offsets and counts are printed in decimal.
.TP
.B \-\^\-hex\-prefix\fR, \fP\-\^\-no\-hex\-prefix
Print (or do not print, the default) \fB0x\fP before hexadecimal offsets.
.TP
.B \-\^\-hex\-suffix\fR, \fP\-\^\-no\-hex\-suffix
Print (or do not print, the default) \fBh\fP after hexadecimal offsets.
.TP
.BR \-p [\fIDIGITS\fP] ", " \-\^\-pad\-output [ =\fIDIGITS\fP ]
Pad offsets with leading zeros: to \fIDIGITS\fP digits if given, otherwise to
the width of the largest offset in the file (for pipes, 4 GiB is assumed).
The argument must be attached (\fB\-p4\fP, not \fB\-p 4\fP).
.TP
.B \-\^\-no\-pad\-output
Do not pad offsets (default).
.SS "Context bytes"
Context is counted in input bytes.  It is printed around the match, set off
with \(lq | \(rq, and is clipped at the start and end of the input (a final
incomplete input byte is not shown).  They are not matched against, and
neighbouring matches may print the same context.  At most 1048576 input bytes of
context in either direction are supported.
.TP
.BI \-B " NUM" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-before\-bytes=" NUM
Also print
.I NUM
bytes before the match.
.TP
.BI \-A " NUM" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-after\-bytes=" NUM
Also print
.I NUM
bytes after the match.
.TP
.BI \-P " K" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-prefix\-num\-bytes=" K
Print as many input bytes before the match as the value of the \fIK\fPth input
byte (1-based) of the matched data.  This follows length fields in the data, whose
size is not known when the pattern is written.  Added to \fB\-B\fP.  Ignored
if the match has fewer than \fIK\fP bytes.
.TP
.BI \-S " K" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-suffix\-num\-bytes=" K
Likewise for bytes after the match.  Added to \fB\-A\fP.
.TP
.BI \-O " \fR[\fP+|\-\fR]\fP" NUM "\fR, \fP\-\^\-off\-by=" [+|\-]NUM
Adjust the counts obtained with \fB\-P\fP and \fB\-S\fP by \fINUM\fP, to allow
for a length field that includes (or excludes) its own header.  \fB+\fP adds;
\fB\-\fP subtracts; with no sign the adjustment is negative.  Counts never go
below zero.
.SS "Byte display and transforms"
.TP
.B \-\^\-ascii
Append a hex-editor style rendering of everything printed, with
non-printable bytes shown as \fB.\fP.  Requires \fB\-\^\-output\-byte\fP of
at most 8.
.TP
.B \-\^\-no\-ascii
Do not (default).
.TP
.BI \-g " NUM" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-group\-size=" NUM
Print output bytes in groups of
.I NUM
output bytes, each group joined together and groups separated by a space (as
.BR xxd (1)
does).  Default 1.  Maximum 4096.  The match, leading context and trailing
context are each grouped separately.
.TP
.B \-\^\-big\-endian\-group
Show the bytes of each group in stream order (default).
.TP
.B \-\^\-little\-endian\-group
Show the bytes of each group last byte first, so a group reads as a
little-endian number.
.TP
.B \-\^\-byte\-reverse\-group
Reverse the order of the bytes shown in each group (applied after the
endian option, so \fB\-\^\-little\-endian\-group \-\^\-byte\-reverse\-group\fP
cancel out).
.TP
.B \-\^\-bit\-reverse\-group
Reverse the order of all the bits in each group (the byte order reverses
and the bits inside each byte reverse).
.TP
.BI \-I " K\fR[\fP,K...\fR]\fP" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-invert\-byte=" K
Print the \fIK\fPth input byte(s) (1-based) of the match with all of their bits
inverted.
May be given more than once.  The ASCII column shows the original bytes.
Positions beyond the match are ignored.
.SS "Miscellaneous"
.TP
.B \-\^\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\^\-version
Print the version and license and exit.
.
.SH "OUTPUT FORMAT"
Each match is printed on its own line as
.PP
.RS
[\fIFILE\fP\fB:\fP][\fIOFFSET\fP\fB:\fP][\fIBEFORE\fP \fB|\fP] \fIBB BB BB\fP... [\fB|\fP \fIAFTER\fP]  [\fB|\fIascii\fB|\fR]
.RE
.PP
where
.I OFFSET
is the (by default hexadecimal) offset of the first matched input byte, with
\fB+\fP\fIBITS\fP appended for a match that starts part-way through one, and
the output bytes follow as hexadecimal values (two digits each by default)
separated by spaces.  The
optional parts appear according to \fB\-H\fP, \fB\-\^\-no\-byte\-offset\fP,
the context options and \fB\-\^\-ascii\fP.
.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
At least one match was found (with \fB\-L\fP: at least one file was listed).
.TP
.B 1
No match was found.
.TP
.B 2
An error occurred (bad option or pattern, unreadable file, ...).  This
takes precedence over 0 and 1, except that \fB\-q\fP exits 0 on a match.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find the string \(lqhello\(rq followed by a NUL byte:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \(aq68 65 6c 6c 6f 00\(aq data.bin
.RE
.PP
List the files in a tree that begin with a PNG signature:
.PP
.RS
.B find . \-type f \-exec bgrep \-lE \(aq^89504e470d0a1a0a\(aq {} +
.RE
.PP
Count ELF headers in a disk image:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-c \(aq7f 45 4c 46\(aq disk.img
.RE
.PP
A record with a length byte at position 3 (\fBAB\fP, length): show the
record header and the bytes the length field announces:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-S 3 \(aq41 42 ..\(aq records.bin
.RE
.PP
The same, where the length counts its own two header bytes (so
subtract 2 from it) and a hex-editor view is wanted:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-\-ascii \-S 3 \-O 2 \(aq41 42 ..\(aq records.bin
.RE
.PP
Show 16 bytes of context on each side of a match, with decimal offsets
padded to the file size:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-\-dec\-mode \-p \-B 16 \-A 16 \(aqde ad be ef\(aq core.dump
.RE
.PP
View 32-bit little-endian words around a pattern:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-g 4 \-\-little\-endian\-group \-A 12 \(aq7f 45 4c 46\(aq a.out
.RE
.PP
Find a bit pattern that straddles a byte boundary (the nibbles
.B 8 6 5
inside \(lq68 65\(rq); the offset is reported as \fB2+4\fP:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-\-half\-byte 865 data.bin
.RE
.PP
Search 12-bit packed samples for the values 0x123 then 0x456, showing the
match as 12-bit values and its position in input bytes:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-\-input\-byte=12 \-\-output\-byte=12 \(aq123 456\(aq samples.bin
.RE
.PP
Find the text \(lqhello\(rq in GSM 7-bit packed data (least significant
bit first) and see it as plain septets:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-\-lsb\-first \-\-input\-byte=7 \-\-output\-byte=7 \-\-ascii \(aq68 65 6c 6c 6f\(aq sms.bin
.RE
.PP
Report where a flag bit pattern occurs, in bits:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-\-binary \-\-align=1 \-\-bit\-offset \(aq1010 0101\(aq dump.bin
.RE
.PP
Search a pipe, showing only the bytes:
.PP
.RS
.B dd if=/dev/sda bs=1M count=64 | bgrep \-\-no\-byte\-offset \(aqff d8 ff ..\(aq
.RE
.PP
Several patterns, from a file:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-f magic.txt firmware.bin
.RE
.
.SH NOTES
.B bgrep
was originally written to examine data from a consumer EEG device.
.
.SH BUGS
In regex mode, if the leftmost-longest match at some position ends on a
character that is not on an input-byte boundary, a shorter aligned match at
the same position is not considered; the search moves on instead.
.PP
Fixed patterns are compared bit by bit when a candidate does not start on a
file-byte boundary, which is slower than the aligned case.
.PP
